reasoning_over_luck.evidence:
The deal is random and matters. My estimate is that roughly 40-50% of the per-deal outcome comes from hand quality. Over 9 deals, though, the result turns on decisions:
- whether and how high to bid, with a secret simultaneous bid against unknown hands;
- the trump choice, where Hearts doubles stakes;
- Flek/Re decisions made after seeing trick 1;
- card play: saving trumps for the last trick, dumping Kings as defender, and when to cash Aces and Tens.

There is plenty to infer. Each player sees 4 of 12 cards, and the announced bids reveal the strength of the other two hands. Every played card is announced, so the remaining 8-card split can be narrowed trick by trick. The stake swings, up to s = 40, happen only through player choices (a high contract, Hearts, Flek, Re), not by chance.

A careless bidder or Flekker loses heavily to a careful one, as in policy (a) above. With equal good play the deals decide, but a strong hand still needs correct bidding and play to cash in, and a weak forced eldest can limit the loss to 2 by bidding 50 in its best non-Heart suit. Good play is clearly favoured.
